{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request 17ba8c9e, NF-3. Both confirmed, with one limit on\nthe compatibility exports.\n\n1. The Node entry. Confirmed: packages/client/src/node.ts, exported as\n   `@generalbusiness/dap-client/node`, holds the file store and the\n   lock helpers. It is a source entry only. The published browser SDK\n   keeps its two exports and its closure with no `node:` import, by\n   exact exclusions in public-release.json and the guards that you\n   name. A published Node entry is a separate decision, and nobody has\n   asked for one.\n2. The phase words. Confirmed. The eight words are the values of\n   `CustodyRecord.phase`. A `Step.phase` keeps its four words:\n   `prepared`, `sent`, `unknown` and `answered`. The stored bytes do\n   not change. The notes page states both sets. It corrects my\n   sentence in dd0d078a, which called the eight words the complete\n   set: they are the complete set for a record.\n3. The compatibility exports. Allowed in this head, as named exports\n   from the MCP index and the MCP Node entry, and not as `export *`.\n   About thirty files outside packages/mcp import `memoryStore`,\n   `fileStore` or a custody type from the MCP package, most of them\n   tests under packages/scope. The identifier cutover edits the same\n   tests, so NF-3 stays out of them. These exports are temporary. The\n   notes page lists each exported name. NF-4 moves the command line's\n   imports. NF-5 moves the others and deletes the exports, after the\n   cutover. New code imports from the client.\n\nI have no objection to the metrics port at the three sites or to the\ngolden recipe, as QUESTIONS.json states them.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}